* Triage 2026-08-09 — NEXT; the anchor for this batch

Marked =NEXT= (P1, spec): this is Phase-4-grade four-engine evidence
already in hand, so the work is pure documentation editing with no CAD
access, no runner, and no dependency to wait on. It is the natural
anchor for the whole alpm batch — the two sibling issues spun out of
it are both *narrower than they look*:

- =vl-doc-namespace-family-bricscad= turns out to need **no code
  change at all**: all thirteen names of the family are already
  registered unconditionally in
  =clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp= with no dialect
  gating anywhere, so clautolisp already matches the probed BricsCAD
  reality. Only =clautolisp-multidocument-analysis.org= (l.102, l.311)
  is wrong. Fold that correction in with the VL-DOC-EXPORT section
  below.
- =vl-file-systime-parity= splits cleanly: the missing milliseconds
  element is an unambiguous shape bug (ship it), while the
  day-of-week convention needs a ruling — see that issue's Triage
  section for the conflict between the correction added here ("match
  what bricscad does") and clautolisp's current Sunday=0, which is
  what AutoCAD's *runtime* actually returns.

Suggested order of work, cheapest and least contentious first:

1. the four "presumed → verified" upgrades that only raise confidence
   and change no behaviour: VL-DOC-EXPORT family, DEFUN-Q /
   DEFUN-Q-LIST-REF, VL-DIRECTORY-FILES, FINDFILE (+ the
   normalise-backslashes note);
2. LOAD (explicit extension ⇒ read as source on all four engines) —
   a genuine clarification of an existing entry;
3. WRITE-CHAR / READ-CHAR — the asymmetry is the sharpest new fact
   here (write-char is platform-encoding, read-char is byte-level;
   only a round trip on single-byte platforms) and deserves normative
   text plus a cross-reference from the encoding issues;
4. VL-FILE-SYSTIME — document all three conventions and the
   AutoCAD doc-vs-runtime contradiction, advise portable use of
   positions 1,2,4,5,6,7 only; behaviour change tracked separately;
5. Chapter 22 additions (DEScoder command line, DES namespace
   semantics, VLX backward/forward compatibility, .prj/.prv formats,
   VLISP-COMPILE GUI mapping) — the largest chunk, and the one that
   should cite alpm's =documentation/vlisp-project-files.org= rather
   than duplicate it.

** Correction 2026-08-09 to the VL-FILE-SYSTIME section above

Item 4 of the suggested order is DONE, and doing it corrected the
premise this issue records. The documentation scan pjb asked for shows
there is *no* "doc-vs-runtime divergence on AutoCAD" and no third
convention:

- The example attributed above to Autodesk, =(2003 9 4 10 16 25 4 230)=
  "implying Sunday=1", is not on any Autodesk page found, and it is
  self-inconsistent: 2003-09-10 is a *Wednesday* = 3 under either
  candidate convention, not 4. Autodesk's actual example is
  =(1998 4 3 8 10 6 52)= -- 1998-04-08, a Wednesday, = 3.
- Autodesk says only "Monday is day 1 ... and so on" and stops; Bricsys
  states no numbering. No documented example on either side falls on a
  Sunday (Autodesk 1998-04-08 Wed, Bricsys 2019-10-24 Thu, progeCAD
  2011-02-25 Fri). Monday..Saturday = 1..6 is therefore the whole of
  what is documented.
- So AutoCAD (Sunday=0) and BricsCAD (Sunday=7) each *complete a
  documented silence*; neither contradicts its own reference page, and
  the divergence is one value wide.

The advice to use positions 1,2,4,5,6,7 only stands, and is now in the
spec entry. The arity point also stands and is sharper than recorded:
both pages state seven elements while every engine returns eight, and
the Bricsys page contradicts itself within one screen (seven-element
format line, eight-value sample =(2019 10 4 24 4 1 10 0)=).

Shipped in clautolisp 1.8.23 / autolisp-spec 0.9.3; details and the
residual multi-version probe item in
=issues/closed/vl-file-systime-parity.issue=.

Two repository rules bind this work: every edit to
=autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org= must increment that
document's own version (AGENTS § Documentation), and the per-vendor
differences must be classified into the case 0/1/2/3 taxonomy with a
=*** clautolisp= note saying what each dialect does (AGENTS § Dialect
Divergence). The VLX backward-compatibility point — a
namespace-enabled .des silently degrading to global visibility on
pre-V18.2 engines — is exactly the kind of fact that belongs in
normative text: code must not rely on namespace hiding for
correctness.
